"note":"Old Irish (Goídelc), the language of the eighth- and ninth-century glosses, the early laws and the sagas, in the Latin letters of the manuscripts, left to right; Ogham is for inscriptions on stone, not for running text, so it isn't used. Spelling is normalised, as the dictionaries print it, with length marks (á, é, í, ó, ú) and the Tironian note ⁊ for ocus 'and', as the scribes wrote it. Old Irish has a large written corpus but few abstract or technical words, so modern ideas are coined from attested roots and used the same way everywhere: cloch (stone) for block, ordugud (ordering) for program, scríbend (writing) for script, rith (running) for run, with Reith! (run!) on the Run button, delb (form, image) for model, gnímaid (doer) for agent, glés (instrument) for tool, scrútan (examination) for review, cétscríbend (first writing) for draft, torad (fruit) for result, claechlaid (thing that changes) for variable, lebrán (little book) for file, comrar (casket) for folder, rímaire (reckoner) for computer, félire (calendar, as in Félire Óengusso) for calendar, sreth (row) for list, cairt (charter) for record, gort (field) for a record's field, lín (net) for the web, duillén (leaf) for page, comartha (sign) for token, eochair (key) for API key, comardae (token, mark) for checkpoint, aithesc (message) for message and epistil (letter) for email. Field names, status values, settings, commands and product names stay as they are, as in the other languages. Exported Python prints the same words.",
